### **Technology selection**

| Layer           | Recommended technology         | Reason                             |
| --------------- | ------------------------------ | ---------------------------------- |
| Frontend        | Next.js                        | Web-first application and SEO      |
| Language        | TypeScript                     | Shared types and safer development |
| Styling         | Tailwind CSS                   | Consistent responsive design       |
| UI components   | shadcn/ui                      | Customizable interface             |
| Backend         | Next.js API / server functions | Initial application logic          |
| Database        | Supabase PostgreSQL            | Relational data and transactions   |
| Authentication  | Supabase Auth                  | Account and session management     |
| Realtime        | Supabase Realtime              | Live participation                 |
| Geolocation     | PostGIS                        | Location-based queries             |
| Cache           | Redis                          | Hot data and rate limiting         |
| Background jobs | Managed queue / worker         | Notifications and scheduled tasks  |
| Storage         | Supabase Storage initially     | User-generated media               |
| Deployment      | Vercel or equivalent           | Web deployment                     |
| Monitoring      | Sentry + product analytics     | Errors and behaviour               |

This is a recommended starting stack, not a requirement to use every service from day one.

### **Important architecture decision**

I would start with a **modular monolith**, not microservices.

That means one deployable backend with well-separated internal modules.

Why?

- Easier to develop.
- Easier to debug.
- Lower infrastructure costs.
- Simpler transactions.
- Less operational complexity.
- Can later extract heavily used services independently.

Do not start with 15 microservices because you want millions of users.

Design for extraction, not premature distribution.

